1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,036
Welcome to Prompt to Production.

2
00:00:02,316 --> 00:00:07,543
This is lesson eleven of the free course, How to Become an AI Application Engineer.

3
00:00:07,823 --> 00:00:11,983
Today, you'll write your first program that talks to a large language model.

4
00:00:12,263 --> 00:00:14,716
And by the end, you'll understand every line of it.

5
00:00:16,116 --> 00:00:18,932
Here's what you'll be able to do by the end of this lesson.

6
00:00:19,382 --> 00:00:23,265
Call an LLM from Python, with your API key stored safely.

7
00:00:23,715 --> 00:00:26,083
Understand each part of the request you send.

8
00:00:26,533 --> 00:00:29,626
Read the response, including the tokens you just paid for.

9
00:00:30,076 --> 00:00:33,703
And work out what each call costs, so you never get a surprise bill.

10
00:00:35,103 --> 00:00:37,791
Before we write code, let's see what actually happens.

11
00:00:38,071 --> 00:00:42,508
Your code uses the provider's SDK, which is just a small library.

12
00:00:42,958 --> 00:00:46,051
The SDK sends a request over HTTPS.

13
00:00:46,331 --> 00:00:53,457
Inside it: which model to use, a system prompt, the conversation messages, and a limit on how long the answer can be.

14
00:00:53,907 --> 00:00:56,403
The provider runs the model on its own servers.

15
00:00:56,683 --> 00:00:58,112
You never download the model.

16
00:00:58,562 --> 00:01:04,941
And you get a response back, with the generated text, the reason the model stopped, and how many tokens were used.

17
00:01:05,221 --> 00:01:05,903
That's it.

18
00:01:06,183 --> 00:01:08,701
The SDK only builds this request for you.

19
00:01:08,981 --> 00:01:10,474
There's no magic in between.

20
00:01:11,874 --> 00:01:13,175
Let's set up the project.

21
00:01:13,455 --> 00:01:16,335
I'm using uv, a fast Python package manager.

22
00:01:16,615 --> 00:01:19,751
Create a new project, and add the Anthropic SDK.

23
00:01:20,201 --> 00:01:23,017
Then put your API key in an environment variable.

24
00:01:23,297 --> 00:01:24,897
Never paste a key into your code.

25
00:01:25,177 --> 00:01:30,297
Code gets committed, pushed and shared, and leaked keys are found and abused within minutes.

26
00:01:31,697 --> 00:01:32,935
Here's the whole program.

27
00:01:33,215 --> 00:01:34,473
First, create a client.

28
00:01:34,753 --> 00:01:37,591
It picks up your key from the environment automatically.

29
00:01:38,041 --> 00:01:39,811
Then call messages dot create.

30
00:01:40,091 --> 00:01:44,294
You choose a model, and max tokens, which caps how long the answer can be.

31
00:01:44,574 --> 00:01:46,537
That also caps what you pay for the answer.

32
00:01:46,987 --> 00:01:51,211
The system prompt sets the model's role and rules for the whole conversation.

33
00:01:51,491 --> 00:01:54,008
The messages list is the conversation itself.

34
00:01:54,288 --> 00:01:56,272
Right now, it's a single user message.

35
00:01:56,722 --> 00:01:59,389
Finally, print the text of the first content block.

36
00:01:59,669 --> 00:02:00,629
That's the answer.

37
00:02:02,029 --> 00:02:02,818
Now run it.

38
00:02:03,098 --> 00:02:05,061
In a second or two, the answer comes back.

39
00:02:05,511 --> 00:02:08,113
Run it again, and you'll probably get a different answer.

40
00:02:08,393 --> 00:02:09,204
That's normal.

41
00:02:09,484 --> 00:02:13,303
The model samples its words, so the output varies from call to call.

42
00:02:13,583 --> 00:02:15,247
Your code has to be ready for that.

43
00:02:16,647 --> 00:02:18,119
Don't just grab the text, though.

44
00:02:18,399 --> 00:02:19,999
The response tells you much more.

45
00:02:20,449 --> 00:02:22,795
Stop reason tells you why the model stopped.

46
00:02:23,075 --> 00:02:24,377
End turn means it finished.

47
00:02:24,657 --> 00:02:27,985
Max tokens means it hit your limit, and the answer was cut off.

48
00:02:28,265 --> 00:02:29,438
Always check this one.

49
00:02:29,888 --> 00:02:32,619
And usage gives you the input and output tokens.

50
00:02:32,899 --> 00:02:36,504
Tokens are pieces of words, and they're exactly what you're billed for.

51
00:02:36,784 --> 00:02:40,304
Professional AI engineers log these numbers from day one.

52
00:02:41,704 --> 00:02:43,432
So let's turn tokens into money.

53
00:02:43,712 --> 00:02:49,685
Providers charge per million tokens, and output tokens usually cost more than input tokens.

54
00:02:49,965 --> 00:02:54,211
Prices change, so copy the current ones from your provider's pricing page.

55
00:02:54,661 --> 00:02:56,346
Then a tiny function does the math.

56
00:02:56,626 --> 00:03:00,743
Multiply each token count by its price, and divide by a million.

57
00:03:01,193 --> 00:03:04,436
A single call like ours costs a tiny fraction of a cent.

58
00:03:04,716 --> 00:03:10,369
But an app with thousands of users makes millions of calls, so this function will matter a lot later.

59
00:03:11,769 --> 00:03:14,628
One last thing, and it surprises almost everyone.

60
00:03:14,908 --> 00:03:16,209
The model has no memory.

61
00:03:16,489 --> 00:03:18,644
Every API call starts from zero.

62
00:03:19,094 --> 00:03:20,459
Tell it your name in one call.

63
00:03:20,739 --> 00:03:22,937
Then, in the next call, ask what your name is.

64
00:03:23,217 --> 00:03:27,163
It has no idea, because the second request never contained your name.

65
00:03:27,613 --> 00:03:29,256
So how do chatbots remember?

66
00:03:29,536 --> 00:03:31,904
That's exactly what we'll build in the next lesson.

67
00:03:33,304 --> 00:03:35,864
Before you go, four beginner mistakes to avoid.

68
00:03:36,314 --> 00:03:38,682
Hardcoding your API key in the source code.

69
00:03:39,132 --> 00:03:42,375
Ignoring the stop reason, and shipping answers that were cut off.

70
00:03:42,825 --> 00:03:46,131
Not tracking tokens, and finding out about cost from your bill.

71
00:03:46,581 --> 00:03:48,885
And assuming the model remembers earlier calls.

72
00:03:49,165 --> 00:03:49,869
It doesn't.

73
00:03:51,269 --> 00:03:59,291
Your homework: make your first call, print the tokens and the cost of every call, and try three different system prompts to see how they change the answer.

74
00:03:59,571 --> 00:04:02,173
The full code is in the GitHub repo linked below.

75
00:04:02,623 --> 00:04:06,634
In the next lesson, we'll give the model a memory, and build a real chatbot.

76
00:04:06,914 --> 00:04:08,365
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

77
00:04:08,645 --> 00:04:09,391
See you there.

